<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<appendix xml:id="grv-app-comparisons">
  <title>Dimension truncations and birational classes</title>
  <introduction><p>The stable birational quotient in <xref ref="grv-ch-birational"/> gives a well-defined detector on the full Grothendieck ring. A tempting alternative is to retain the leading birational classes in each dimension. This works in a truncated scissors group, but higher-dimensional scissors relations can destroy that information. We prove the truncated statement and use the Calabi–Yau construction of <xref ref="grv-thm-imou"/> to exhibit the obstruction in the full ring.</p></introduction>
  <section xml:id="grv-app-filtration">
    <title>Truncated scissors groups</title>
    <p>For <m>d\ge0</m>, write <m>K_{\le d}=K_0(\Var_k^{\le d})</m> for the abelian group generated by varieties of dimension at most <m>d</m>, with scissors relations imposed only among those varieties. Set <m>K_{\le-1}=0</m>. Inclusion of generators defines maps <m>i_{d-1}:K_{\le d-1}\to K_{\le d}</m>. Unlike the original Grothendieck ring, a single truncated group has no product of arbitrary generators within the same dimension bound.</p>
    <p>Let <m>\mathrm{Bir}_d(k)</m> be the set of birational classes of integral <m>d</m>-dimensional varieties. Sending a variety to the sum of the birational classes of its <m>d</m>-dimensional irreducible components defines a homomorphism
      <md number="no">b_d:K_{\le d}\longrightarrow\Z[\mathrm{Bir}_d(k)].</md>
      Here the target is the free abelian group on that set. For a closed-open decomposition, each top-dimensional component contributes its birational class on exactly one side, so the assignment respects the relations.</p>
    <proposition xml:id="grv-prop-truncated-birational">
      <statement><p>The sequence
        <md number="no">K_{\le d-1}\xrightarrow{i_{d-1}}K_{\le d}\xrightarrow{b_d}\Z[\mathrm{Bir}_d(k)]\longrightarrow0</md>
        is exact. No injectivity assertion for <m>i_{d-1}</m> is included.</p></statement>
      <proof><p>Every birational class has an integral representative, so <m>b_d</m> is surjective. Classes of smaller-dimensional varieties map to zero. To prove the reverse containment, first remove the intersections of the top-dimensional irreducible components of a variety, together with its smaller-dimensional components. This expresses its class as a sum of integral dense open pieces and a class represented in smaller dimension.</p>
        <p>Now write an element of <m>\ker b_d</m> as a difference of two such sums. Within each birational class, the positive and negative top-dimensional pieces occur with the same multiplicity. Pair them. Birational integral varieties have isomorphic dense open subsets; subtracting the classes of these common opens expresses the difference of each pair using its lower-dimensional complements. The entire element is therefore in <m>\operatorname{im}i_{d-1}</m>. For <m>d=0</m>, the complements are empty and the same argument applies with <m>K_{\le-1}=0</m>.</p></proof>
    </proposition>
    <p>Thus <m>K_{\le d}/\operatorname{im}i_{d-1}</m> is free on birational classes. The use of the image is essential. Relations available in <m>K_{\le d}</m> may already identify elements that were distinct in <m>K_{\le d-1}</m>.</p>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="grv-app-full-filtration">
    <title>Passing to the full Grothendieck ring</title>
    <p>Let <m>F_d\Rk</m> be the image of <m>K_{\le d}\to\Rk</m>. These images form the dimension filtration. The preceding proposition induces a surjection
      <md number="no">\Z[\mathrm{Bir}_d(k)]\longrightarrow F_d\Rk/F_{d-1}\Rk.</md>
      The target also imposes every relation coming from higher-dimensional varieties in the full ring. Consequently the truncated proposition does not assert that this surjection is an isomorphism. Lin–Shinder describe the transition kernels in terms of invariants of birational maps <xref ref="grv-bib-LinShinder" detail="Proposition 2.10 and Remark 2.11"/>.</p>
    <proposition xml:id="grv-prop-dimension-kernel">
      <statement><p>For the threefolds <m>X,Y</m> of <xref ref="grv-thm-imou"/>, the element
        <md number="no">\delta=[X\times\A^1]-[Y\times\A^1]\in K_{\le4}</md>
        is nonzero, but has zero image in <m>K_{\le5}</m> and hence in <m>\Rk</m>. In particular, the surjection from <m>\Z[\mathrm{Bir}_4(k)]</m> to <m>F_4\Rk/F_3\Rk</m> is not injective.</p></statement>
      <proof><p>The fourfolds <m>X\times\A^1</m> and <m>Y\times\A^1</m> are not birational, since such a birational map would make <m>X,Y</m> stably birational. Their two basis elements in <m>\Z[\mathrm{Bir}_4(k)]</m> are therefore distinct. Their difference is <m>b_4(\delta)</m>, proving <m>\delta\ne0</m> in <m>K_{\le4}</m>.</p>
        <p>Write <m>X_1=X</m>, <m>X_2=Y</m>, and let <m>E_i</m> be the exceptional divisors in the double-blowup construction of <xref ref="grv-sec-affine-line-zero-divisor"/>. Its closed-open decompositions give
          <md number="no">[D]-[E_i]=[F_i]-[X_i].</md>
          Here <m>D,F_i</m> have dimension five, <m>E_i</m> have dimension four, and <m>X_i</m> have dimension three. A finite bundle-trivializing stratification of <m>X_i</m> computes
          <md number="no">[E_i]=[X_i]+[X_i\times\A^1]</md>
          using varieties of dimension at most four. The Bruhat cells of either <m>F_i</m> have dimensions <m>0,\ldots,5</m>, so <m>[F_1]=[F_2]</m> already in <m>K_{\le5}</m>. Subtracting the two equations now gives <m>\delta=0</m> there. The six-dimensional flag variety supplies the construction of <m>D</m>, but its class is not used. The nonzero element <m>b_4(\delta)</m> consequently maps to zero in the associated graded of the full ring.</p></proof>
    </proposition>
    <p>This example explains the choice of nonvanishing arguments in the main chapters. Leading birational classes can distinguish elements in a truncated presentation, but an invariant of the full ring must also respect higher-dimensional relations. The Hodge polynomial and the stable birational quotient do so by their construction.</p>
  </section>
</appendix>
